          deepDive: "**〜にもかかわらず** = *in spite of, despite, even though*. The book: it means 「〜なのに」 — the actual outcome differs from what the situation would lead you to expect. It's the formal, objective counterpart of **〜のに**: 雨なのに → 雨にもかかわらず. Literally, にかかわらず (*without being tied to*) + も (*even*).\n\nConnection (as in the book):\n- **V-ている / V-た + にもかかわらず**: 収入が減少しているにもかかわらず (*although incomes are falling*), 警備を強化したにもかかわらず (*even though security had been tightened*).\n- **N + にもかかわらず**: 努力にもかかわらず, 授業中にもかかわらず, 高熱にもかかわらず, 雨にもかかわらず.\n- ＊ note: **なA / N + である + にもかかわらず** (フリーソフトであるにもかかわらず *although it's free software*) and **いA + にもかかわらず** (値段が高いにもかかわらず *despite being expensive*).\n- Sentence-initial: **それにもかかわらず** *nevertheless*.\n\nThe second half reports a **real outcome** that goes against expectation: 業績はよくならなかった, 貯金額は増加している, 45万本も売れた. It can carry surprise or criticism (授業中にもかかわらず、学生はおしゃべりしている *even though class is in session, the students are chatting*), but more restrained than のに.\n\nConstraints:\n- No commands, requests or intentions in the second half: ✗雨にもかかわらず来てください → ✓雨でも来てください (*please come even if it rains*).\n- のに can trail off at the end of a sentence with regret (来てくれればよかったのに); にもかかわらず can't.\n\nIn speeches it's a staple of polite thanks: 本日は雨にもかかわらず / お忙しいにもかかわらず、お集まりいただき… (*thank you for coming despite the rain / your busy schedules*).\n\nThe big trap is **〜にかかわらず** (#5), without も: *regardless of* (☞ p.223 〜にかかわらず). このテーマパークは天候にかかわらずお楽しみいただけます (*you can enjoy the park whatever the weather*) vs 悪天候にもかかわらず、マラソン大会は予定通り行われた (*the marathon was held as planned despite the bad weather*). にかかわらず = the same in every case; にもかかわらず = one actual case turned out against expectation.\n\nOther neighbors:\n- **〜ながら（も）** (#15): *although*, prefers the same subject and a state: 若いながらも立派だ.\n- **〜ものの** (#39): *although (true), but…* — admits a fact, then a disappointing result.\n- **〜つつも** (#112): literary *although*, with verbs of knowing or thinking.\n- **〜わりに（は）** (#95): *considering ~* — the result is more or less than expected by degree, not a contradiction: 値段のわりにおいしい (*tasty for the price*).\n- **〜からといって**: *just because ~ (doesn't mean)* — needs a negative conclusion.\n\nJLPT tip: if the blank follows a fact and the second half reports something surprising or disappointing that actually happened, choose にもかかわらず: 学校の規則で禁止されているにもかかわらず、彼はバイクで通学している (*he rides a motorbike to school even though the rules forbid it*). If it follows a range noun (天候, 年齢) and states a general rule, choose にかかわらず.",
          see: [5, 15, 39, 95, 112],
          index: ["〜にもかかわらず", "Nにもかかわらず", "それにもかかわらず", "であるにもかかわらず"],
